friends and family of congressman john  lewis are gathering in atlanta  this morning for the funeral of the  civil rights icon former presidents  barack obama  george w bush and bill clinton along  with house speaker nancy pelosi  quite a distinguished lineup are set to  speak at the historic ebenezer baptist  church today  the church as you've heard has played a  key role in the civil rights movement  the reverend dr martin luther king jr  served as a co-pastor with his father  until his death in 1968 reverend rafael  warnock is now the senior pastor of the  church and will preside over this  morning ceremony he is also  a democratic candidate for the u.s  senate reverend good morning it's so  nice to see you this morning  listen the nation has been paying  tribute to the congressman  all week but today is a final farewell  and you are doing the honors  how would you describe what do you want  us to remember and how would you  describe your friend  good morning gail it's great to be here  with you  one of the great honors of my life has  been to serve as the senior pastor  to john lewis a true american patriot  who sacrificed it all because he  believed in the promise  of our american democracy and at the  heart of his activism  and his work as a public servant uh was  his faith and so  it's a it's fitting that we would be  here uh on this day  uh his work and his life's mission and  his perspective began  at church in troy alabama and  we're grateful to be with him and and  his family as he's crossed that final  bridge  into eternity yeah he walked people know  him say he walked and lived his faith  when did you all first meet for the  first time and what do you remember  about him  yes i i first met john lewis as a  student  at morehouse college some of the  students had planned a protest i was  president of the chapel assistance at  the martin luther king jr international  chapel on the campus of morehouse  college  and we invited some of the influencers  and leaders in our  community to join the students in the  protest we invited  city council persons and others john  lewis is the only one who showed up  he was a newly elected congressman i  remember how inspired we were as  students to hear his voice but we were  more inspired just by the the power of  his presence  uh this icon in american history yeah  you know reverend when you said showing  up  i said what that must mean to you to be  his pastor but you talked about him  showing up  we interviewed the clintons last week  and they said the thing that stood out  for them about john lewis is that  he was seemingly always there and he  always showed up hillary clinton tells a  story about him  showing up at a book signing at costco  and he said he just happened to be in  the neighborhood  that was who this man was  that's certainly who he was and history  will remember him because he  showed up in the places where it really  counts  he was just captured by this whole idea  of freedom it captured his imagination  the voice of martin luther king jr  caught his attention as a young man and  uh he reached out to dr king  and forever will his deeds be etched in  history but the thing that i want to  emphasize  is that at the heart of his activism  really was his faith a faith passed on  from enslaved ancestors and so when you  see a man stand up to jim clark  when you see someone go on a bridge  knowing that they are facing  violence and brutality he did that  because his faith  represented a kind of spiritual power  shaped by  suffering a moral audacity that  transcended human station  and called on human law to more closely  align itself with the law of love all of  us are better  because john lewis came our way as a  young man he wrestled with a call to  ministry  but instead of preaching sermons he in a  real sense became a sermon  reverend how was he able to do this with  no bitterness he talked  about being beaten he talked about  almost losing his life he had been  arrested several times  we often say he loved the country when  they didn't love him back and he had no  bitterness how was he able to do that  well you know he he often said that that  he didn't that well dr king used to say  that love is is too heavy a burden to  bear  and john lewis internalized that he  believed it  and here we are all of these days later  john lewis passed away almost a couple  of weeks ago  and there's so much going on in the news  cycle and yet a whole nation  pauses to remember him it's because he  wasn't a transactional politician he was  a transformational figure  who brought all that he could bring  to the promise of democracy he kept his  eye on the prize  i think he say to me all the time keep  your eyes on the prize yeah he left a  great lesson for all of us you know did  you see his op-ed this morning in the  new york times  that this is something that stuck out  when you see something that's not right  you must say something  you must do something democracy is not a  state  does an act in each generation must do  his part to help build what we called  a beloved community  i love that part that's john lewis  that's john lewis and we've got to keep  his work moving  forward a lot of folks are celebrating  him right now but the best way to honor  him really  is to reauthorize the voting rights act  that was his life's work and some of  those who celebrate him right now  are sitting on the voting rights act  they ought to pass it and honor this  true american patriot  a lot a lot of people are hoping that  happens thank you very much reverend  warnick we'll be thinking about you  today and watching thank you so much sir
 

